## Why This Is Asked
Asynchronous order processing with idempotency is common in production; testing must prove duplicates don't create multiple orders and failures route to DLQs with retries, plus end-to-end visibility.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys, exactly-once processing
- Message queues (Kafka), DLQ design
- Retry/backoff strategies, consumer failure modes
- End-to-end tracing and correlation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test (pseudo)
const request = require('supertest');
const api = require('supertest')('https://api.example.com');
const idKey = 'order-uniq-123';

test('duplicate submissions do not create duplicates', async () => {
  const r1 = await api.post('/orders').set('Idempotency-Key', idKey).send({items:[{sku:'A1',qty:1}]});
  const r2 = await api.post('/orders').set('Idempotency-Key', idKey).send({items:[{sku:'A1',qty:1}]});
  expect(r1.status).toBe(202);
  expect(r2.status).toBe(202);
  // fetch status after processing
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate DLQ contents and retry semantics across multiple partitions?
- What observability would you add to prove end-to-end visibility across services?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a real-world asynchronous export flow requiring idempotency, retry logic, and observability. It validates high-concurrency behavior and failure modes across the message broker and downstream analytics partner.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent POST endpoints with deduplication keys
- At-least-once vs exactly-once processing semantics
- End-to-end observability and monitoring
- Exponential backoff retry strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test skeleton for idempotent export
async function testIdempotentExport(exportId) {
  const res1 = await post('/exports', {
    user_id: 'user123',
    export_options: { format: 'csv', date_range: '30d' },
    export_id: exportId
  });

  const res2 = await post('/exports', {
    user_id: 'user123',
    export_options: { format: 'csv', date_range: '30d' },
    export_id: exportId
  });

  // First request should queue the export
  assert(res1.status === 202);
  // Second request should return existing export
  assert(res2.status === 200 && res2.data.export_id === exportId);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for long-running, tenant-scoped APIs must guarantee exactly-once semantics while handling retries and high concurrency. This question probes idempotency handling, job deduplication, cross-tenant isolation, and failure resilience in a realistic pipeline.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys and deduplication
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics
- Background job orchestration and status visibility
- Concurrency across tenants and race-condition handling
- Failure modes and recovery (worker crash, DB outages)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test snippet (Node.js with axios)
const axios = require('axios');
async function testIdempotentPost() {
  const payload = { tenant: 'ace', params: { range: '30d' } };
  const id = 'idt-guest-123';
  const r1 = await axios.post('/reports', payload, { headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': id } });
  const r2 = await axios.post('/reports', payload, { headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': id } });
  console.assert(r1.data.jobId === r2.data.jobId, 'Dedup should return same job-id');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate end-to-end latency distribution under increasing load?
- How would you verify audit logs reflect idempotent operation and tenant boundaries?
